The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(x,y)&=2 - 4 x \\ f_y(x,y)&=2 y - 1 \\ \nabla f(1,2)&=\left(-2\right)\,i+\left(3\right)\,j \\ \|\nabla f(1,2)\|&=\sqrt{\left(-2\right)^2+\left(3\right)^2} \\ &=\sqrt{13}
\end{aligned}
$$

This gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:

$$
\begin{aligned}\mathbf n&=\frac{\nabla f(1,2)}{\|\nabla f(1,2)\|} \\ &=\frac{\left(-2\right)\,i+\left(3\right)\,j}{\sqrt{13}} \\ &=\left(- \frac{2 \sqrt{13}}{13}\right)\,i+\left(\frac{3 \sqrt{13}}{13}\right)\,j
\end{aligned}
$$

Its negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.

The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Here the logarithm has positive argument $3$, so the function is differentiable near the point.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(x,y)&=- y + \frac{1}{x - 2 y} \\ f_y(x,y)&=- x - \frac{2}{x - 2 y} \\ \nabla f(7,2)&=\left(- \frac{5}{3}\right)\,i+\left(- \frac{23}{3}\right)\,j \\ \|\nabla f(7,2)\|&=\sqrt{\left(- \frac{5}{3}\right)^2+\left(- \frac{23}{3}\right)^2} \\ &=\frac{\sqrt{554}}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

This gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:

$$
\begin{aligned}\mathbf n&=\frac{\nabla f(7,2)}{\|\nabla f(7,2)\|} \\ &=\frac{\left(- \frac{5}{3}\right)\,i+\left(- \frac{23}{3}\right)\,j}{\frac{\sqrt{554}}{3}} \\ &=\left(- \frac{5 \sqrt{554}}{554}\right)\,i+\left(- \frac{23 \sqrt{554}}{554}\right)\,j
\end{aligned}
$$

Its negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.

The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(x,y)&=\left(- \sin{\left(x + y \right)} + \cos{\left(x + y \right)}\right) e^{- x + y} \\ f_y(x,y)&=\left(\sin{\left(x + y \right)} + \cos{\left(x + y \right)}\right) e^{- x + y} \\ \sin\left(- \frac{\pi}{6}\right)&=- \frac{1}{2} \\ \cos\left(- \frac{\pi}{6}\right)&=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \\ \nabla f(- \frac{\pi}{6},0)&=\frac{e^{\frac{\pi}{6}}}{2}\,\mathbf v \\ \mathbf v&=\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)\,i+\left(-1 + \sqrt{3}\right)\,j \\ \|\mathbf v\|^2&=\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)^2+\left(-1 + \sqrt{3}\right)^2 \\ &=8 \\ \|\nabla f(- \frac{\pi}{6},0)\|&=\sqrt{2} e^{\frac{\pi}{6}}
\end{aligned}
$$

This gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:

$$
\begin{aligned}\mathbf n&=\frac{\nabla f(- \frac{\pi}{6},0)}{\|\nabla f(- \frac{\pi}{6},0)\|} \\ &=\frac{\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)\,i+\left(-1 + \sqrt{3}\right)\,j}{2 \sqrt{2}} \\ &=\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{4} + \frac{\sqrt{6}}{4}\right)\,i+\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4} + \frac{\sqrt{6}}{4}\right)\,j
\end{aligned}
$$

Its negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.
